+# UI host
+
+An extension can provide the session UI instead of drawing inside Pi's terminal UI. It registers a synchronous provider, interactive mode starts no `InteractiveMode`, and the provider's context becomes `ctx.ui` for every extension. Pi retains ownership of the session lifecycle, shutdown, and signal handling.
+
+```ts
+import * as sdk from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
+
+export default function (pi) {
+  if (sdk.UI_HOST_API_VERSION !== 2 || typeof pi.registerUIHost !== "function") {
+    pi.on("session_start", (_event, ctx) => {
+      ctx.ui.notify("This frontend needs patched Pi or its launch wrapper", "error");
+    });
+    return;
+  }
+  pi.registerUIHost(({ session, actions }) => frontend.attach(session, actions).ui);
+  pi.on("session_start", () => frontend.start());
+  pi.on("session_shutdown", () => frontend.close());
+}
+```
+
+Namespace-probe optional exports instead of naming a missing export in an import: a missing named export can fail before feature detection runs.
+
+```json
+{ "pi": { "uiHost": true, "extensions": ["extensions/index.ts"] } }
+```
+
+`pi.uiHost` in package.json puts this extension first in load order. A declaration in the module body cannot establish ordering because Pi would have to import the module first. A host registered without manifest priority still works, but Pi warns that earlier extensions missed its module substitutions. Print, JSON, and RPC modes ignore the UI host registration.
+
+## Provider context
+
+`UI_HOST_API_VERSION` is `2`. The provider receives `{ session, actions }`: the currently owned `AgentSession`, and the bound `ExtensionCommandContextActions` when a mode supplied them. Interactive host mode supplies the command actions. Both handles belong to the current session; retain neither across session replacement.
+
+Pi asks the provider during extension binding, before `session_start`, and again after session replacement or `/reload`. The provider must return `ExtensionUIContext` synchronously. Attach observers and construct the UI there, but start listeners or connectors in `session_start`. Release them idempotently in `session_shutdown`. Close only frontend-owned resources: the extension must not dispose Pi's session or launch a replacement Pi runtime.
+
+`session.subscribe()` observes structured events and `session.prompt()` submits input. `actions` supplies `newSession`, `fork`, `switchSession`, `navigateTree`, `waitForIdle`, and `reload`. Session replacement operations belong to user actions, not lifecycle handlers, where they can deadlock. See [SDK](sdk.md) for session ownership and subscription semantics.
+
+Pi waits for a shutdown request or signal, then disposes its runtime and stops theme watching. `ctx.shutdown()` requests that shutdown; the frontend does not call `process.exit()`. Active operations are aborted by Pi before disposal.
+
+## Module substitution
+
+`registerExtensionModule(specifier, module)` replaces a specifier for extensions imported after registration, not already-imported graphs. Register during the extension factory and declare manifest priority. A second registration replaces the first; `unregisterExtensionModule(specifier)` removes the override.
+
+Substitution is implemented through the extension loader's virtual module map, not global Node hooks. Pi's own imports and an extension's independent `createRequire()` calls are unaffected. Unregister frontend-owned substitutions during shutdown so reload imports start from stock modules again.
+
+## Limits
+
+- There is no terminal UI. Terminal shortcuts, built-in terminal-only commands, and `ctx.ui.terminal` are unavailable. The host implements or rejects custom component factories itself.
+- The extension still sees `ctx.mode === "tui"`; this signals UI availability, not the existence of a local terminal renderer.
+- Setup, trust, and missing-working-directory prompts that occur before extension binding remain terminal-only.
+- Multiple hosts use the first registered host. Removing a host on reload does not start a terminal UI automatically.
